Attack angles probed live against scripts/whatif-verify.sh port 8128
(:8080 never touched; torn down cleanly at the end):
- Sweep endpoint (/whatif/conversion-sweep — builds engine input via
  buildEngineInput → prepare.From) and /whatif/montecarlo: both return 200
  with a dormant entry present (horizon 20, entry at year 25). Confirmed by
  reading buildEngineInput — it funnels through the same prepare.From /
  ValidateOneTimeExpenses choke point as the page handler, so dormancy-safety
  is structural, not per-endpoint.
- Reactivation exactness (30→add year 25→shrink 20 [dormant, hint shown,
  page 200]→regrow 30 [hint gone, page 200]): same ID, same Amount (50000)
  persisted throughout. OneTimeExpensesForYear recomputes
  `e.Amount * compoundedFactorFromPercent(s.InflationRate, currentYear*12)`
  fresh from static fields on every call — no cached/mutated inflation state
  exists to double-apply, so "doubly inflated" or "re-ID'd on reactivation"
  is structurally impossible, not just untested.
- UI hint truthfulness: onetime-card.html uses `{{if ge .Item.Year
  .Settings.ProjectionYears}}` — exact boundary match. The OOB flow
  (whatif-results-with-oob → #onetime-list hx-swap-oob) is wired for the
  mutating /whatif/settings handler (used by the projection-years slider),
  so the hint appears/disappears live on horizon shrink/regrow without a
  manual reload — verified by curl: hint text present in the same POST
  response body that shrinks the horizon, absent in the regrow response.
- Handler-local check honesty: uses `retirementMgr.Load()` (a verified real
  deep copy per attempt-2's checker) read fresh inside the handler, not a
  stale value — confirmed by reading the code, current.ProjectionYears is
  the just-loaded value.
- Huge year (1e9) at add time: rejected by the same `year >=
  ProjectionYears` handler check (any realistic ProjectionYears is far
  smaller), so a permanently-dormant huge-year entry cannot be created
  through the add form; OneTimeExpensesForYear is only ever invoked by the
  stepper for currentYear in [0, ProjectionYears), so even a huge Year value
  already on disk (e.g. from direct file editing) can never reach the
  compounding call, structurally.
- amount=1e400 (out-of-range float): strconv.ParseFloat returns a range
  error, correctly rejected with 400, not silently coerced to +Inf.

Residual finding (not failing the contract): amount=NaN or amount=Inf
submitted directly via HTTP (curl, not reachable by typing into the
`type="number"` input in a real browser) passes both the handler's
`amount < 0` check and ValidateOneTimeExpenses's `e.Amount < 0` check
(NaN/Inf comparisons against 0 are false in Go), then fails downstream at
`json.Marshal` (JSON has no NaN/Inf representation) with a leaked internal
error string and an HTTP 500. Verified live: NOT persisted (settings file
unchanged), and GET /whatif still returns 200 immediately after — so it is
recoverable, not bricking, and cannot corrupt persisted money math (Go's
JSON encoder is a hard backstop against ever writing NaN/Inf to disk from
any write path). Also verified pre-existing/precedented: the same amount=NaN
probe against /whatif/expense (a different, out-of-scope form) 500s
identically, confirming this is a shared, already-shipped quirk of
parseRequiredFormFloat/statusForMutationError, not something T18 introduced.
Per the dispatch brief's severity-class test (normal user action ->
unrecoverable/500, or silent money corruption) this does not qualify: the
browser's number input blocks non-numeric entry, so it requires bypassing
the UI, and neither corruption nor bricking follows. Noted for awareness,
not a contract-class defect.

No remaining path found that errors on a dormant entry across
render/projection/sweep/MC/backtest/tax-optimizer paths; dormancy is exact
